#### On \(dN/dy\) and \(\langle p_{T}\rangle\)

The triton yield measured at mid-rapidity (\(|y|<0.5\)) is calculated from the measured \(p_{T}\) range and extrapolated to the unmeasured regions with various parameterizations. The percentage contribution to the yields on extrapolation is about 10-20%, unmeasured regions of \(p_{T}\) are additional source of systematic error. The extrapolation is done by the individual blast-wave model fits [67].

\[\frac{1}{2\pi p_{T}}\frac{d^{2}N}{dp_{T}dy}\propto\int_{0}^{R}rdrm _{T}I_{0}\left(\frac{p_{T}\sinh\rho}{T}\right)K_{1}\left(\frac{m_{T}\cosh\rho}{ T}\right) \tag{3.5.3}\] \[\rho=\tanh^{-1}\beta_{r}\] \[\beta_{r}(r)=\beta\left(\frac{r}{R}\right)^{n}\]

where the extracted fit parameters are kinetic freeze-out temperature \(T_{\rm kin}\), average radial flow velocity \(\langle\beta\rangle\), and the flow velocity profile exponent \(n\).

The systematic uncertainties on the extrapolated yields are estimated by comparing the extrapolation with those using other fit functions. Another fit function is

\[\mbox{Double exponential} : A_{1}\exp(-p_{T}^{2}/T_{1}^{2})+A_{2}\exp(-p_{T}^{2}/T_{2}^{2}) \tag{3.5.4}\]

The systematic uncertainties on the extrapolated total yields are dominated by the uncertainties in the extrapolation, which are estimated to be 10-20% of the extrapolated part of the integrated yields for triton. The 5% overall MC uncertainty is added in quadrature.

